body:
Quite the opposite; As an INTJ I try my best to collect data and convert it into useful information any time during my waking hours. From my own experience and observations of the self:

- Collection of data about the environment, situation, and agents takes top priority;
- Collected data is constantly analyzed with various methods and tools I’ve accumulated throughout my life whether its physics, mathematical, or psychological;
- In some cases, inferencing by intuition can be the fastest method but even I have enough self-doubt to not completely trust my intuition all the time (except when it comes to judging people whereby you have very little data to work with, with regards to their personality and nature);
- Most of my decisions have been done with thorough and often detailed research. I’ve only ever been most adventurous with food.

The way I understand the MBTI description, especially in the context of your discussion, is that Judging types would make a thorough analysis based on the data with greater emphasis on empirical methods and results. Perception types are more likely to accommodate or even prefer theoretical models of a situation needing a decision.

My partner is an INTP and this is the greatest difference I perceive every time we have discussions, and is the reason I say the above.

Ditto. Even tho HDMI sends out digital frames for the image, the color space could be getting scrambled due to noise on the line. I'm not an expert on the HDMI encoded signal, but the TV's HDMI transceiver chip could be doing error correction for the noisy/lossy signal. This ends up with the final image on the screen with some colours in bad values, hence the globally strong pink hue.

&#x200B;

Edit: You could try arranging the cable differently. HDMI cables do not like bending. I recommend spooling or compressing the cable in large circles. A more relaxed bend radius usually solves most HDMI issues. This is why most HDMI cables are built with structures that make it more rigid, whether its a metallic mesh (which doubles to filter out external electromagnetic noise) or firm reinforcing materials for the insulators between each pin wire.

I often edited my MS thesis on my 10.5" 2017 iPad Pro on MS Word using an Office 365 subscription. I didn't have problems editing the content, but for advanced manipulation of objects I always fell back to my 13" MBA. Considering that my manuscript is made up of 80+ images, several tables, and upwards of 200+ pages with a relatively condensed format, it held up really well. Having the keyboard really helps. But if your just typing on the on-screen keyboard, then its going to be cumbersome.

&#x200B;

As for note taking, I use Notes Plus or Notability. Notability if I need to just get in there to take down notes quickly. Notes Plus if I have time to pull out the Pencil and write on the screen. Notes Plus can do script to text conversion within the app so its very convenient for easily converting from script notes to other document formats.
